{"id": 6, "ts": 1779615993.9957225, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "Based on the evidence we have so far, Candidate\u202fB stands out for operational reliability (very conscientious) and stress resilience (handles stress very well), as well as solid technical competence (weather assessment and computer skills). However, the interview notes also flag serious concerns for crew cooperation and professional communication: B \u201ccan be uncooperative\u201d and \u201ccan be grumpy,\u201d and he makes \u201cnasty remarks\u201d about colleagues. These issues could undermine cockpit teamwork on long\u2011distance flights.\nCompared with the other candidates:\n- Candidate\u202fA shows strong situational awareness and leadership but is perceived as a \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d and \u201cnot open to new ideas,\u201d raising doubts about adaptability and crew harmony.\n- Candidate\u202fC demonstrates quick decision\u2011making, calmness in crises, and good technical understanding, yet struggles with communication, is seen as egocentric, and is reluctant to further his education\u2014concerns for crew cooperation and adaptability.\n- Candidate\u202fD excels in problem\u2011solving, concentration, and responsibility, but is described as a \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d with a \u201chot temper,\u201d which also threatens crew cooperation and professional communication.\nGiven the balance of strengths and weaknesses, my provisional vote is for **Candidate\u202fB**, provided we can address the cooperation and communication concerns (e.g., through targeted behavioral training). I would like to confirm whether any additional observations exist regarding Candidate\u202fD\u2019s crew\u2011cooperation and Candidate\u202fA\u2019s reliability to ensure we have a complete picture before finalizing.", "thoughts": "We have just asked Agent 1 and got answer. Now we need to produce public message: we should incorporate this info, maybe compare